SwiftSheets — third-party material

SwiftSheets itself is MIT-licensed (see LICENSE). The repository also carries material from other projects:

1. numbers-parser (https://github.com/masaccio/numbers-parser), MIT License, Copyright 2021 Jon Connell.
   Used, unmodified or machine-derived, for Apple Numbers support (spec chapters 10–11, Appendix B.8):
   - Sources/SheetNumbers/Resources/fonts.json — the PostScript-font-name → family map (numbers-parser
     `generated/fontmap.py`), which Numbers needs in both directions: it names a font "HelveticaNeue-Bold"
     where the model names the family "Helvetica Neue"
   - Sources/SheetNumbers/Resources/constants.json — the integer constants Apple declared as plain int32 in the
     Protobuf, so the schema carries no names for them (number-format kinds, text alignment, duration styles),
     extracted from numbers_parser.constants / numbers_parser.cell
   - Sources/SheetNumbers/Resources/registry.json — the IWA type-id → message-name registry (numbers-parser
     `generated/mapping.py`), extracted by scripts/extract-numbers-schema.py
   - Sources/SheetNumbers/Resources/functions.json — the Numbers function-id → name table (`generated/functionmap.py`)
   - Sources/SheetNumbers/Resources/schema.json — field numbers / types of the Protobuf messages SwiftSheets reads
     and writes, extracted from the descriptors embedded in numbers-parser's generated `*_pb2.py` modules
     (themselves derived from the Numbers application with numbers-parser's proto-dump tooling)
   - Sources/SheetNumbers/Resources/empty.numbers — the empty document used as the write template
   - Tests/SwiftSheetsTests/Fixtures/numbers/*.numbers — test documents from numbers-parser's test suite, except
     conditional-formats-15.numbers, links-notes-15.numbers, chart-and-control-15.numbers, popup-15.numbers,
     controls-15.numbers, array-15.numbers, stock-15.numbers, pivot-mixed-15.numbers, category-15.numbers,
     filter-15.numbers and stockcell-15.numbers, which were produced by Numbers 15.3.1 on this maintainer's own
     Mac (three from workbooks built with openpyxl, which is MIT; controls-15 and stock-15 by driving Numbers
     over AppleScript; pivot-mixed-15 from a workbook SwiftSheets itself wrote; the last three by hand in the
     Numbers UI — see MAINTENANCE.md)
   No Python code from numbers-parser is included; the Swift reader / writer were written against the extracted
   schema and the observed behaviour of the documents.

2. openpyxl (https://foss.heptapod.net/openpyxl/openpyxl), MIT License, © 2010-2024 openpyxl.
   - Tests/SwiftSheetsTests/Fixtures/openpyxl/ — test fixture files, unmodified (see NOTICE.md there)
   - Tests/OpenpyxlParity/openpyxl-3.1.5-tests.json — an enumeration of openpyxl's test function names

The Numbers file format is proprietary to Apple and undocumented. Reading and writing it for interoperability is
generally accepted practice, but this is not legal advice; embedding SwiftSheets' Numbers support in a commercial
product is the integrator's own call.
